INT. NATURAL HISTORY MUSEUM - TAXIDERMY COLD STORE - NIGHT

A walk-in freezer the size of a school gym. Steel racks of stuffed animals wrapped in clear plastic: a bear, a pair of okapi, a flock of sealed albatrosses. The hum of the compressors is constant.

YEVGENY BRODSKY (40s, quilted jacket, frost on his eyebrows) kneels at a floor safe. A stethoscope hangs around his neck. He turns the dial a quarter click at a time.

BASIL ACHTERBERG (60s, museum night guard's uniform, crisp, keys at his belt) stands over him with a thermos. He pours tea into the cap and offers it down.

YEVGENY
I'm good.

BASIL
It's minus eighteen. You're shivering.

YEVGENY
I'm concentrating.

Yevgeny doesn't look up. Basil sips the tea himself. Looks at a polar bear in its plastic shroud, nods to it like an old neighbour.

BASIL
You like the quiet?

YEVGENY
I like it when nobody talks.

BASIL
He used to say that. Your father.

Yevgeny's hand stops. Just for a heartbeat. The dial doesn't move.

YEVGENY
You said you didn't know him.

BASIL
I said I didn't know him well.

He screws the cap back on the thermos. His expression is mild, neighbourly. Yevgeny resumes the dial. Click. Click. Click.

The safe clunks open. Inside, a velvet bag. Yevgeny lifts it, weighs it, loosens the drawstring. A heavy stone slides into his glove: a rough diamond the size of a plum.

YEVGENY (CONT'D)
(low)
There you are.

BASIL
It's smaller than I thought.

YEVGENY
It's always smaller.

BASIL
Put it back.

Yevgeny looks up. The thermos is still in Basil's right hand. In his left, steady as a stone, is a small black pistol.

YEVGENY
Basil.

BASIL
Put it back in the bag. Pull the string. Slide the bag back on the shelf. Close the door.

YEVGENY
We said sixty-forty.

BASIL
We did.

YEVGENY
You said you were retiring.

BASIL
I am. Tonight.

Yevgeny draws the string slowly. Laying the bag in the safe, his hand lingers. Frost creeps up his sleeve.

YEVGENY
How long have you known?

BASIL
He told me what you'd come for. Before he died. He asked me to stand here.

Yevgeny closes the safe door. The sound is small and final. He sits back on his heels, hands visible on his knees.

YEVGENY
He said that?

BASIL
He said, my boy will want to be a thief. Be there when he does.

A long silence. The compressors hum.

Basil lowers the pistol. Sets it on the nearest rack, next to the polar bear. Unscrews the thermos. Pours another cap of tea.

He holds it out. This time Yevgeny takes it.

FADE OUT.
